; SPDX-License-Identifier: LicenseRef-Nordic-5-Clause
;
; IronSide TE - PSA Initial Attestation token format (CDDL, RFC 8610).
;
; The wire format of the token psa_initial_attest_get_token() returns, per
; RFC 9783 ("PSA Attestation Token"). This file is the contract: decode a token
; with a CBOR parser against the shapes below, looking claims up BY LABEL.
;
; Do not decode a token by byte offset. Every claim's position depends on the
; claim set, and the claim set has changed in every release that added a claim.
; A parser that searches by label survives those changes; a hardcoded offset
; does not, and fails silently - it reads a well-formed value from the wrong
; place. The client header (include/ironside_client/psa/initial_attestation.h)
; publishes the labels and the value lengths for exactly this reason, and
; publishes no offsets.
;
; Two properties of the encoding a decoder may rely on, and one it may not:
;
;   - The claims map is DEFINITE-LENGTH, and so is every string and array in
;     the token. A decoder needs no indefinite-length support.
;   - The claims map holds exactly the 9 entries below, with no duplicates.
;   - Map entry ORDER is not part of this contract. CBOR does not require
;     canonical key ordering and RFC 9783 imposes none, so a decoder MUST
;     search by label rather than assume a position. As it happens the nonce is
;     emitted last today; that is an encoder detail and not something to
;     depend on.
;
; The token size is fixed for a given challenge size (620 bytes at a 64-byte
; challenge - PSA_INITIAL_ATTEST_MAX_TOKEN_SIZE), which is why some claims are
; rendered fixed-width below. That is a buffer-sizing guarantee, not an
; invitation to index into the token.

start = psa-attestation-token

; ------------------------------------------------------------------------
; COSE_Sign1 envelope (RFC 9052), tagged. ES256 over the claims map.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; The signature covers the COSE Sig_structure
;   ["Signature1", protected, external_aad, payload]
; where external_aad is a zero-length bstr. A verifier reconstructs it from the
; PROTECTED and PAYLOAD bstrs AS ENCODED - the enclosing byte strings, not the
; values inside them - which is what a CBOR parser hands back for a bstr, so no
; byte arithmetic over the token is needed. Verify with the device's Identity
; Attestation Key (IAK) public part, registered at manufacturing time and found
; by the instance-id claim.

psa-attestation-token = #6.18([
  protected   : bstr .cbor protected-header,
  unprotected : empty-map,
  payload     : bstr .cbor claims-map,
  signature   : bstr .size 64,   ; ECDSA P-256 raw r || s, not DER
])

protected-header = { 1 => -7 }   ; alg: ES256 (ECDSA w/ SHA-256)
empty-map = {}

; ------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The claims map.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; Seven claims RFC 9783 marks MUST, one it marks MAY (boot-seed), and one
; vendor claim (device-uuid). Nothing else. A claim absent here is absent from
; the token - notably certification-reference and
; verification-service-indicator, which this profile omits.

; The entries are LISTED in the order IronSide currently emits them, purely so
; that a code generator producing an order-sensitive decoder (zcbor's default,
; for one) works against a real token without being re-ordered by hand. It is a
; convenience, not a promise: see the note at the top of this file, and search
; by label in anything you write yourself.
claims-map = {
  client-id           => client-id-claim,
  instance-id         => instance-id-claim,
  implementation-id   => implementation-id-claim,
  security-lifecycle  => security-lifecycle-claim,
  software-components => software-components-claim,
  profile-definition  => profile-definition-claim,
  device-uuid         => device-uuid-claim,
  boot-seed           => boot-seed-claim,
  nonce               => nonce-claim,
}

; --- nonce (EAT label 10) -----------------------------------------------
;
; The challenge the caller passed to psa_initial_attest_get_token(), verbatim.
; A verifier holds it against the value it issued; that is what makes the token
; non-replayable. One of the three PSA-legal sizes, and it is the size the
; caller sent - the token does not choose it.

nonce = 10
nonce-claim = bstr .size 32 / bstr .size 48 / bstr .size 64

; --- instance-id / EAT ueid (label 256) ---------------------------------
;
; 0x01 (UEID type RAND) || SHA-256(IAK public key). Identifies the attestation
; key, and so in IronSide TE also the device: a device has one IAK for its
; lifetime. Strip the leading type byte to get the registry lookup key.
;
; A device re-keyed at RMA attests under a NEW instance-id while remaining the
; same die. Use the device-uuid claim to name the die.

instance-id = 256
instance-id-claim = bstr .size 33

; --- implementation-id (PSA label 2396) ---------------------------------
;
; Names the PSA RoT implementation - which part, and which version of
; IronSide's immutable boot stage (IROT). Identifies the IMPLEMENTATION, not
; the instance: every device of one part, silicon revision and IROT version
; reports the same 32 bytes. No per-die register is an input.
;
; READ THE SCHEME VERSION FIRST. A later layout takes a new number rather than
; reinterpreting this one, so a reader that finds a number it does not know
; must treat the 32 bytes as opaque. Ignore the reserved tail rather than
; requiring a value: a later release may name a field there, and every field
; ahead of it keeps its meaning.
;
; The client header publishes these as PSA_INITIAL_ATTEST_IMPL_ID_* byte
; offsets, which are offsets INTO THIS VALUE and not into the token - a CBOR
; parser hands back the 32 bytes and does not resolve their internals.
; scripts/lib/impl_id.py composes and decodes the same bytes host side.
;
;   byte  0     scheme version, currently 1
;   bytes 1-4   FICR.INFO.PART      (big-endian)
;   bytes 5-8   FICR.INFO.VARIANT   (big-endian; four ASCII chars on a part
;                                    whose VARIANT is printable)
;   bytes 9-12  FICR.INFO.CONFIGID  (big-endian)
;   bytes 13-14 FICR.INFO.RAM  in kB (big-endian)
;   bytes 15-16 FICR.INFO.RRAM in kB (big-endian)
;   bytes 17-20 IROT version: major, minor, patch, tweak
;   bytes 21-31 reserved, written zero, ignore on read

implementation-id = 2396
implementation-id-claim = bstr .size 32

; --- client-id (PSA label 2394) -----------------------------------------
;
; The security domain of the caller. RFC 9783 section 4.1.2 gives negative ids
; to callers outside the secure processing environment and forbids 0. It is
; always -1 here: IronSide TE has one non-secure security domain, and both
; non-secure images (the App and IronSide DFU) are that domain, so the claim
; does not say which of them asked.
;
; RFC 9783 section 4.1.2 puts the duty of CHECKING this claim on the verifier.
; A verification path that ignores it is incomplete however well the signature
; verifies.

client-id = 2394
client-id-claim = -1

; --- security-lifecycle (PSA label 2395) --------------------------------
;
; The device's PSA lifecycle state. Only the four DEPLOYABLE states can appear:
; a device that resolves to UNKNOWN (0x0000) or DECOMMISSIONED (0x6000) is
; issued no token at all and gets PSA_ERROR_NOT_PERMITTED, so seeing either
; value in a token is a device-side regression rather than a device state.
;
; Encoded as a CBOR unsigned integer. The client header spells the values as
; PSA_LIFECYCLE_*.

security-lifecycle = 2395
security-lifecycle-claim =
    0x1000  ; ASSEMBLY_AND_TEST
  / 0x2000  ; PSA_ROT_PROVISIONING
  / 0x3000  ; SECURED
  / 0x4000  ; NON_PSA_ROT_DEBUG

; --- profile-definition (EAT label 265) ---------------------------------
;
; The claim-set profile this token follows, which a PSA verification service
; dispatches on. RFC 9783 section 4.5.2.1 fixes the spelling: the fragment is
; appended to the "tag:psacertified.org,2023:psa" tag URI.
;
; The trailing hyphens are padding, and they are deliberate: they hold the
; value at 43 bytes, which is what keeps the token size fixed. Match the value
; exactly, padding included.

profile-definition = 265
profile-definition-claim = "tag:psacertified.org,2023:psa#ironside-----"

; --- software-components (PSA label 2399) -------------------------------
;
; One record per image IronSide TE measures: always all three, always UROT then
; the App then IronSide DFU. Search a record by its measurement-type rather
; than by array position if you care which image you are reading.
;
; The measurement algorithm is not a per-record field. It is SHA-256 for the
; whole token, fixed by the ES256 algorithm in the protected header.
;
; RFC 9783's measurement-description (key 6) is omitted.

software-components = 2399
software-components-claim = [ 3*3 software-component ]

software-component = {
  measurement-type  => sw-measurement-type,
  measurement-value => sw-measurement-value,
  version           => sw-version,
  signer-id         => sw-signer-id,
}

measurement-type = 1
; "UROT", "APP" or "DFU".
sw-measurement-type = "UROT" / "APP" / "DFU"

measurement-value = 2
; SHA-256 over the image's signed region - header, body and protected TLVs,
; exactly the bytes IronSide verifies before booting the image - so a reference
; value comes straight off the released artifact. A slot holding no parseable
; image measures as the hash of zero bytes.
sw-measurement-value = bstr .size 32

version = 4
; The image header's own version, rendered FIXED-WIDTH as "NNN.NNN.NNN+NNN"
; (zero-padded, e.g. "001.002.003+004"), which is what keeps the token size
; fixed. Strip the zero padding to compare against a version printed the
; ordinary way; scripts/lib/mcuboot_image.py prints the same version unpadded
; as "{major}.{minor}.{patch}+{tweak}".
sw-version = tstr .size 15

signer-id = 5
; SHA-256 of the 32-byte key record this image's signature was checked against.
; The record is itself a digest: for App and DFU the SHA-256 of the ECDSA P-384
; public key, and for UROT the SHA-256 of its ECDSA P-384 and ML-DSA-65 public keys.
sw-signer-id = bstr .size 32

; --- boot-seed (EAT label 268) ------------------------------------------
;
; A property of the BOOT SESSION rather than of the device or its images: the
; same value in every token one boot session emits, and a different value in
; every other boot session's. Two tokens carrying the same boot seed are two
; reports about one run; different seeds mean a reset happened in between,
; which the nonce cannot express - it binds a token to one challenge, not two
; tokens to each other.
;
; It is PUBLIC. It travels in the clear in every token, and RFC 9711 section
; 4.2.13 is normative that a boot seed is not a secret and MUST NOT be used
; where a secret seed is needed, such as seeding a random number generator. It
; labels a boot session; it is not key material.

boot-seed = 268
boot-seed-claim = bstr .size 32

; --- device-uuid (vendor label -1000000) --------------------------------
;
; The 128-bit unique identifier of the die this token came off: FICR.INFO.UUID,
; each of the four words little-endian, which makes the value byte-for-byte the
; ADAC soc_id TLV for the same device. Hold a token against `adac.py discovery`
; output directly.
;
; The label is in the CWT claim registry's Private Use range (RFC 8392 section
; 9.1), so a verifier that only knows RFC 9783 skips it. The block
; -1000000..-1000999 is reserved for further Nordic claims.
;
; Unlike instance-id this survives IAK rotation, so it is what names the die
; across an RMA re-key.
;
; WARNING: it is a stable, unauthenticated-read device identifier present in
; EVERY token, so a token is linkable across sessions and across IAK rotation
; by anyone who sees one. The nonce makes tokens non-replayable, not
; unlinkable, and the service applies no access control - any non-secure caller
; may ask. A deployment that needs unlinkable attestation cannot use this claim
; set.

device-uuid = -1000000
device-uuid-claim = bstr .size 16
